  <h2>Definition</h2>
  <p>Agentic Universal Basic Income (AUBI) is a decentralized economic model in which individuals generate income through autonomous AI agents operating on their behalf in digital markets.</p>
  <p>Unlike traditional Universal Basic Income (UBI), which relies on centralized redistribution, AUBI is infrastructure-driven. It uses blockchain networks, self-sovereign identity systems, privacy-preserving cryptography, and smart contracts to enable AI agents to transact, negotiate, and monetize digital activity.</p>
  <p>In simple terms: AUBI allows AI agents to create economic value for individuals within decentralized Web3 environments.</p>

  <h2>How is AUBI different from traditional Universal Basic Income?</h2>
  <p>Traditional UBI is government funded, tax distributed, centralized and policy dependent. Agentic Universal Basic Income is market driven, blockchain enabled, privacy preserving, agent mediated and infrastructure based.</p>
  <p>AUBI does not redistribute existing wealth. It creates new income flows through programmable digital markets powered by AI agents.</p>

  <h2>How does Agentic Universal Basic Income work?</h2>
  <p>AUBI depends on five foundational infrastructure layers:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Self-Sovereign Identity (SSI).</strong> Individuals maintain control of digital identity through decentralized identifiers (DIDs) and verifiable credentials. This enables AI agents to act with delegated authority without relying on centralized platforms.</li>
    <li><strong>Privacy-preserving cryptography.</strong> Zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) and post-quantum cryptographic systems ensure that data can be verified without being exposed. This protects individual sovereignty while enabling trust in decentralized environments.</li>
    <li><strong>Tokenized data vaults.</strong> Personal data becomes an asset stored in encrypted vaults. AI agents can license access to this data under programmable consent frameworks.</li>
    <li><strong>Smart contracts and micro-payments.</strong> Blockchain Layer-1 and Layer-2 networks enable secure, low-cost transactions. Smart contracts automate compensation when agents execute value-generating activities.</li>
    <li><strong>AI agents as economic participants.</strong> Autonomous AI agents operate within decentralized finance (DeFi) systems and digital asset marketplaces to negotiate, transact, and optimise outcomes on behalf of individuals.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why Agentic Universal Basic Income matters</h2>
  <p>Artificial intelligence is expected to displace significant categories of labor. As automation scales, traditional wage-based income structures face structural pressure.</p>
  <p>AUBI proposes an alternative. Individuals retain ownership of identity and data. AI agents generate income autonomously. Digital asset markets enable programmable value exchange. Decentralized infrastructure replaces centralized extraction models.</p>
  <p>This represents a shift from employment-based income to agent-mediated digital productivity.</p>

  <h2>The role of blockchain and Web3 infrastructure</h2>
  <p>AUBI is not possible without blockchain infrastructure. Blockchain Layer-1 networks provide security and settlement. Layer-2 scaling solutions enable transaction efficiency. Smart contracts automate compensation. Web3 identity systems enable decentralized authentication.</p>
  <p>Together, these components create the technical foundation for the agentic economy.</p>

  <h2>Is AUBI a policy proposal or a market infrastructure?</h2>
  <p>AUBI is an infrastructure thesis, not a government welfare proposal. It is rooted in digital sovereignty, self-sovereign identity, cryptographic privacy, decentralized marketplaces and agent-based automation.</p>
  <p>It can coexist with traditional economic systems but operates independently of state redistribution.</p>

  <h2>Who is AUBI relevant for?</h2>
  <p>AI startups building agent frameworks, blockchain Layer-1 and Layer-2 developers, Web3 identity platforms, digital asset infrastructure providers, policy makers exploring AI governance, and institutional investors evaluating agentic markets.</p>
